WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Your eyes are extremely sensitive.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.040
So when dealing with eye injuries,

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.440
our first and most important rule is

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.400
never rub an injured eye.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.680
Rubbing can push a foreign object deeper

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.880
or cause it to scratch the surface of the eye,

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.880
making the injury much worse.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.459
As you assess the patient, ask them a few key questions.

00:00:18.459 --> 00:00:20.200
Did anything hit your eye?

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.480
Does it feel like something is still in there?

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.760
Can you open your eye and can you still see well?

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.160
If someone has a foreign object like dust

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.200
or a small piece of debris in their eye

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.040
and blinking doesn't clear it,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.560
your next step is to rinse the eye with clean water.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.200
If you see an item stuck under an eyelid,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.959
you can gently use sterile gauze to lift the lid

00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:42.939
and remove the visible debris.

00:00:42.939 --> 00:00:45.000
However, if the object is embedded

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.959
or you simply cannot get it out,

00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:48.560
stop what you are doing.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.759
Cover both eyes to prevent movement

00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:53.439
and wait for EMS to arrive.

00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:55.680
When you are dealing with chemical injuries,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.400
time is of the essence.

00:00:57.439 --> 00:00:59.599
You must rinse the eye with clean water

00:00:59.599 --> 00:01:02.680
for at least 15 to 20 minutes without stopping.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.559
When rinsing, position the head

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:07.480
so the water flows from the inner corner of the eye

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.199
toward the outer corner.

00:01:09.199 --> 00:01:10.599
This prevents the chemicals

00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:13.000
from washing across the bridge of the nose

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.239
and into the unaffected eye.

00:01:15.239 --> 00:01:17.360
If the person is wearing contact lenses

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.120
and can remove them easily,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.360
they should do so while you continue the flushing process.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.080
For a physical hit to the eye,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.080
apply a cold pack to the area

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.360
to help reduce pain and swelling.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.400
Call 911 immediately if they are experiencing severe pain,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.440
have any changes in their vision,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.639
or if a chemical or object won't come out.

